\par }{\b\fs24 Modifiers to Dice Roll}{\fs24 
\par   -2 if the attitude of the law is persecutory
\par   -1 if the attitude of the law is hassling
\par   +1 if the law is corrupt or tolerant
\par   -1 if the merchants attitude is one of warfare
\par   +2 if the merchants are submissive or infiltrated
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Income Multipliers (see below)
\par }{\fs24 Easygoing operations: x 0.5
\par Routine operations: x 0.8
\par Average operations: x 1.0
\par Pushy operations: x 1.5
\par Aggressive operations: x 2.0
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Levels of Operations
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab \tab These are referred to in the multipliers following Table 23, above. They refer to the following instructions given by the guildmaster:
\par    \tab }{\i\fs24 Easygoing:}{\fs24  Guildmembers take life very easy. No morale modifiers.
\par    \tab }{\i\fs24 Routine:}{\fs24  Guildmembers take life fairly easy, but keep their hands in. A +1 modifier applies to morale for the first month (after this some of them get bored and touchy).
\par    \tab }{\i\fs24 Average:}{\fs24  The standard level of operations, no effects on morale.
\par   }{\i\fs24  \tab Pushy:}{\fs24  The guildmembers are pushed into doing some extra jobs here and there, and grand, ambitious activities are eagerly pursued. This gives +1 to morale for the f
irst month (excitement), but -1 per month after the first if sustained, cumulative to a maximum of -4).
\par    \tab }{\i\fs24 Aggressive:}{\fs24  Guildmembers are told by the guildmaster to steal anything which isn't bolted to the floor. This has an immediate effect on morale of -2
, to which -1 is added per month (cumulative) to a maximum of -10; at this point, a revolt is automatically triggered.
\par    \tab The more active the guild is, the more likely thieves are to be apprehended by the law, however!
\par    \tab The sums of money gained by the 
guildmaster this way will not be great, unless the guild is a large one, the community is rich, and/or the guildmaster uses aggressive thieving tactics. This is likely to be ale money for guildmaster-level thieves, so other activities must be used to gene
rate income. These will need planning by the player in consultation with the DM. Reference should be made to the }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24  when considering the money side\emdash 
how much shopkeepers can afford to pay as protection money, for example. These sums are usually lower than most players think! Making sure the target one strikes at is rich is a shrewd step in any thiefly activity.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 The Long Arm of the Law
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab \tab A monthly roll is made on Table 24 to determine whether there has been a confrontation with the law.
\par }{\b\fs24 
